Two different answers, because they're different kinds of curve.
Circle involute — yes, and the fit is better suited than the catenary
The involute has a property that makes it a natural PH target: its speed is a polynomial.
P(t) = a(cos t + t·sin t,  sin t − t·cos t)
P′(t) = a·t·(cos t, sin t)          speed = a·t     tangent angle = t
Speed linear in t, tangent angle equal to the parameter. So a chain of PH cubics fitted through points and tangents converges exactly as it does for the catenary:
cubics	worst error (a = 1, t ≤ 3)
1	0.73
2	0.040
4	0.0057
8	0.0004
Nothing in the machinery was catenary-specific — from_hermite only ever wanted two points and two tangents. It'll fit any curve you can sample, and for the involute it's fitting a curve whose own speed law is already polynomial, so there's no square root to chase even in principle.
Bouncing ball — no PH needed, and that's the more interesting answer
A parabola is exactly a quadratic Bézier — not an approximation:
(x₀, x₀²) · ((x₀+x₁)/2, x₀x₁) · (x₁, x₁²)      error 1.8e-15
So a bouncing ball isn't a PH job at all; it's a chain of degree-2 curves meeting at corners, not tangents. Every bounce is G0: the ball's direction reverses instantly, so the join has no tangent to match. PH cubics would be overkill and slightly wrong — you'd be smoothing a corner that must stay sharp.
Where it does get interesting for your work is the heights. With elasticity e, the apexes are H, e²H, e⁴H, … — a geometric progression, the same shape as the doubling stacks. So a bouncing ball is:
- arcs: exact quadratics, integer control points if you choose the bounce points on the lattice
- heights: a geometric sequence, one ratio, like the p-triple doubling
- joins: corners, which is precisely what the cusp/corner machinery in the spine layer already handles
In other words, the ball is closer to your chain work than to the PH work: a sequence of exact integer triangles (the parabolas' control polygons) with deliberate corners between them.
