Short answer: I can’t find a published identification of the hip/vary-form template as a specific logarithmic spiral. The patternmaking literature names the tools — hip curve for hiplines, vary form for armholes and necklines — but does not give their geometry. The templates are proprietary shapes, not published curves.
What is published is the mathematical family that contains the log spiral:
The formula
A logarithmic (equiangular) spiral:
r(θ) = a e^(bθ)
x(θ) = a e^(bθ) cos θ
y(θ) = a e^(bθ) sin θ
b is the shape; a is only scale.
The angle between the tangent and the radius is constant: tan ψ = b.
Radius of curvature: ρ = r √(1 + b²).
Arc length: s = (√(1 + b²) / b)(r − r₀) — so ρ is proportional to s.
Scaling by k is a rotation: Δθ = ln(k) / b. That is why a 126% copy is the same spiral, not a different one.
The general log-aesthetic curve (Miura, Harada) is:
ρ^α = c₀ s + c₁
α = 1 → logarithmic spiral (ρ ∝ s)
α = −1 → clothoid (ρ ∝ 1/s)
other α → the intermediate family.
So “which one?” has two answers to measure: α (is it a log spiral, a clothoid, or between?) and b (which log spiral).
How to identify it from the template
Trace the drawing edge as points.
Estimate curvature ρ and arc length s along it.
Fit log ρ against log s:
slope +1 → log spiral, α = 1
slope −1 → clothoid, α = −1
other slope → log-aesthetic with α = 1/slope.
If it is a log spiral, fit b from ln r = ln a + bθ after finding the pole, or from the constant tangent–radius angle.
Trace the small and large pieces separately. If they give the same b, they are the same design scaled; the 1.26 ratio is then a rotation of ln(1.26)/b, and the join is exact — not G², but the same curve.
The catch with the vary form
A single log spiral has no inflection; it only tightens or loosens. The vary-form curve is often a compound shape (it changes direction for armholes/necklines), so it may be two log-aesthetic segments or an α that changes sign. The hip curve, being one long gentle arc, is the more likely single log spiral. That is a difference in shape, not just size — measure both.
I found no source that publishes the manufacturer’s equation; the honest route is the fit above. If you scan the hip curve and the vary form, I can run the log ρ–log s fit and give you α and b, and tell you whether the 1.26 family is exact for your set.
