I'm here. Roughly right in spirit, but the ratio isn't a rule and the end isn't literally “every chord.” The measured pipeline says:
 	1° step	0.5° step
chain chords, right half	115	221
structural spans (the study's 11 parts)	11	11
chords per structural span	~10.5	~20
fitted cubics in the half	351	352
fitted cubics in the band	376	732
C commands in the whole SVG (with mirror)	1,078	1,436
So the corrections:
1. “1 in 10 at the beginning” is a coincidence of the 1° run. The structural span count is 11 because study 01 has 11 parts, separated by corners and curvature reversals. That number does not change when you refine the step. The chord-to-span ratio therefore scales with the step: about 1 in 10 at 1°, 1 in 20 at 0.5°, 1 in 77 at 0.125°. It is not a fixed decimation; the span boundaries are chosen by how much the path has turned (a 50° budget, plus reversals), not by taking every tenth chord.
2. The end does not use every chord as a cubic either — and the two strokes behave differently.
- The half is gentle: 115 chords and 221 chords both fit to about 351 cubics. The fitter is driven by the 0.02-unit tolerance and the shape, so extra chords get absorbed; the ink count barely moves.
- The band is tight (radius 25): it needs roughly one cubic per chord — 362 chords gave 376 cubics, 722 gave 732. There the “every chord” intuition is close to true.
- The finished SVG is therefore about 1,100–1,450 cubic commands, not 852 chords and not 852 cubics.
3. The raw chords are the plan, not the ink. They define the exact polygon the plan layer believes in. The engine re-fits that shape into cubics at print tolerance, merging where it can and splitting where curvature is tight. Nothing writes the 852-point polygon to the page.
So the honest one-line version is: the chain is dense at the beginning; the structural spans are few and fixed by turns; the end is re-fitted by tolerance — merged on the gentle half, roughly one cubic per chord on the tight band. Your “1 in 10” is the 1° case of the middle row, not a property of the pipeline.
