Terminology first: the datum is now a node framework — a point plus the direction the line is travelling. The list below says "frame" in the old sense; read it as framework.
1. Place one cap at a terminal framework.  
A cap is a finished end from the engine — 36 of them in out/caps, each a small curve with a known anchor point and angle. A terminal is an end recovered from the old ornaments: 150 of them, stored as curves plus, now, the seam point and outward direction. Placing a cap means putting the engine's end onto a recovered end. "Measure that the cap's anchor sits on the seam" means: transform the cap into the terminal's node framework, then check the distance between the two points. It should be zero or a float rounding; if it is not, the framework is wrong. This is the first time the two halves of the project actually touch — caps were made by the engine, terminals were recovered from the old drawings.
2. Place a terminal at a spine node framework.  
The same operation backwards: take a recovered end and hang it on a node of a Pythagorean chain. It matters because it proves the framework is not a special case invented for caps. If both directions work, then "a point and a direction" is a general attachment rule, and any part can meet any other part there.
3. Promotion gate, step 1 — clothoid_lab imports a chain and builds a Piece/Trace from its anchors.  
Today the main package does not use the spine layer at all. This step means the tool that draws ornaments can take a chain, read its points and directions, and make a Piece with a Trace — a full line with a width. Until that import exists, "central" is an intention; after it, the spine is part of the tool.
4. Gate, step 2 — render one spine on the 200 × 250 sheet, +y up, through svg.document.  
Draw the chain at the real page size, in the page's convention (y increases upward), using the existing SVG writer. It has to go through the same output path as every other drawing, or it is a picture in a side file rather than part of the system.
5. Gate, step 3 — run piece_clearance() on a spine and declare one contact.  
The clearance checker measures the smallest gap between strokes and flags crossings, allowing one only where a Contact declares it legal. Running it on a spine shows the new curve is measured by the same rule as everything else, and that declaring a touch works.
6. Gate, step 4 — draw one W03 study entirely from Pythagorean chains.  
W03 is the fleur grammar; study 01 is its first target. "Entirely" means every stroke of that study is built from chains — no engine spans, no hand-drawn cubics. That is the end-to-end proof: a real ornament, drawn from the new curve.
7. Float rails — a float-native outline with a reported residual.  
Rails are the two edges that give a spine its width. They are currently exact for small chains; at 20,000 chords the exact arithmetic is too expensive. This item means computing the outline in floating point and reporting how far it drifts from the exact answer, so width is as usable at scale as the centre line already is. Without that, long chains can be drawn but not given a reliable width.
Items 1–2 are joins between parts. Items 3–6 are the four conditions in context/PLAN.md that make the spine layer officially part of the tool. Item 7 is the scale problem that remains after the curve itself was made cheap.

