1. Rational powers — not strictly integer, but the exactness line falls elsewhere
The exact machinery never needed k^p to be an integer power. It needs the turn at each node to be a whole number of triangle angles, because the heading is Dir^k — a power of one fixed rational unit vector. So the real condition is:
turn_k ∈ ℤ      (then the heading is an exact rational rotation)
k^p with integer p is just the one schedule where that is true by construction. So:
