I'm here. Two different senses of "exact", and I've been sloppy about which is which.
Bertolazzi
It computes the clothoid, to about 1e-15 relative — machine precision. It isn't "exact" in the mathematical sense either: the clothoid has no closed form, so the library evaluates Fresnel-type integrals by series. What it is, is the definition, evaluated as accurately as a double allows. There is no other curve it could be wrong about.
Ours
We compute a different curve — the Euler polygon — and we compute it exactly in a different domain: rational anchors, integer turns, exact closure. So:
vs the ideal clothoid	Bertolazzi	spine
heading at nodes	exact by definition	1e-13°
curvature	exact by definition	≤ 1.4 % of peak
position	0	~1 chord
algebra	transcendental, 16 digits	exact in ℚ, one rounding at output
So no, we are not "1.4 % off Bertolazzi". We are not on that axis at all. Bertolazzi's error is numerical; ours is a modelling difference — a chord polygon standing in for a spiral.
And one correction on the 1.4 %
That figure is absolute, normalised by the peak curvature. Since κ starts at zero, 1.4 % of peak is a large relative error near the beginning of the sweep and a small one near the apsis. It's the right number for "how far does the pen force stray from the ideal line", not for "how wrong is it at every point".
The position figure is the honest headline: one chord, about 3 mm on a 200 mm page. Superimposed, you'd see it. Side by side, you wouldn't.
The point
The spine isn't a cheap Bertolazzi and shouldn't be judged as one. It is exact at something Bertolazzi cannot be: the anchors are rational, the closure is an integer identity, and a fork shares a provable point and vector. Bertolazzi is exact at something the spine cannot be: it is the spiral.
Which is why the handover says use each for its own job.

